ation in  turkish after three months than i did in  czech after six months even though i had  had russian and therefore was familiar  with  the grammar and the structure of slavic  languages whereas turkish is completely  different from anything i've experienced  before and the reason is the mini  stories and the fact that link is faster  better more convenient  now so the idea is with the many stories  is it pounds the language into you  and gets you used to the language now  you're in a better position to observe  what's happening by say looking up  grammar rules or i happen to have bought  for croatian this asimil guide  the conversation which is very well done  and i'm going to get more of these  because once you have invested in the  mini stories in pounding the language  into you  of course that doesn't mean that you can  just turn the language on at any time  um i've had the experience i've said i  studied ukrainian for a long time  without many stories but listen to a lot  of material in ukrainian  but then spent two weeks working on the  um slovak mini stories in link when i  got to the  polyglot conference in bratislava i  couldn't speak ukrainian i could a week  later i was in levi speaking ukrainian  without a problem however well with with  problems but able to communicate but the  slovak effort had pushed the ukrainian  aside and i know today that uh if i were  to meet someone who speaks czech or  polish even though i put a lot of effort  into those languages i can't produce it  given a few hours i'd be able to produce  it  same even more so with greek and  romanian  but i know that in those languages i  have  pounded the language into me at one  stage i did  and so that's where i'm very curious to  see i think these little conversations  are very good because they have a lot of  the sort of basic conversational things  that you would need in a variety of  situations which all by themselves would  be there very difficult to remember  however  if they a