ause  the movies produced in in egypt are not  in standard arabic so  i i don't know how i'm going to do that  but the goal is that i will sort of go  back to the language arabic for three  months then after that persian spend a  week to get back to where i was and then  i have to plow forward to get to that  next level  and maybe  these little conversation guides can be  useful for me when i need to quickly  refresh for a particular situation of  course in standard arabic there's no  no place where people uh speak naturally  in that language  [Music]  this is part of my sort of interest in  the whole polyglot thing in schools that  teach one language  uh typically well not everywhere but in  japan they teach english and canadian  english language schools they teach  french and vice versa and i think we are  capable of learning more than one  language  whatever we invest in learning that  language stays with us  all right  uh even if we can't remember it  it's we have invested in that it's there  uh the mini stories are still there if i  want to use them to refresh  uh so that the fact that i can't produce  it immediately  um  doesn't bother me it's a fact of life  those languages that i know well  i can produce them but the languages  that i haven't achieved that level that  i haven't spoken enough it's very  difficult for me to  suddenly generate them right  but this is therefore an experiment so  can i over a period of let's say two  years or so  working  on three languages  separately  get them all to a level where i feel i  can comfortably speak in other words  close to a b2 level i don't know if that  can be done but that's the experiment  one of the things that i think is going  in my favor is  every time you're trying to learn  something  and you're staying with it there comes a  point where you have a sense of  frustration you have a sense that you  aren't learning anything  you're beating your head against the  brick wall  so if i go three months with one 